1
00:00:02,900 --> 00:00:06,660
It's goat bone? I don't want that.

2
00:00:06,700 --> 00:00:09,820
And it's got a cockerel on it.

3
00:00:09,860 --> 00:00:11,820
Yeah, I'll have one of them
and the kettle.

4
00:00:11,860 --> 00:00:13,940
Oh, God!  What?

5
00:00:13,980 --> 00:00:15,580
My bed's gone out of alignment.

6
00:00:18,020 --> 00:00:20,980
'Our adventure began
in Uganda where,

7
00:00:21,020 --> 00:00:23,580
'armed with three
£1,500 estate cars,

8
00:00:23,620 --> 00:00:27,380
'we were given
a spectacular challenge.'

9
00:00:27,420 --> 00:00:30,420
You will find
the source of the River Nile.

10
00:00:34,100 --> 00:00:38,100
'To make life easier, we converted
our cars into mobile homes.'

11
00:00:38,140 --> 00:00:39,340
Whoa!

12
00:00:39,380 --> 00:00:42,180
What's that? Living quarters
combined with a workshop.

13
00:00:46,540 --> 00:00:48,900
Nobody in the history of Africa

14
00:00:48,940 --> 00:00:52,340
has ever been
more comfortable than this.

15
00:00:52,380 --> 00:00:56,020
'Having failed to find the source
of the Nile in western Uganda...'

16
00:00:57,180 --> 00:01:00,580
Southeast?  Yeah. It's no good.

17
00:01:00,620 --> 00:01:04,340
'..we decided the actual source
was in northern Tanzania.'

18
00:01:04,380 --> 00:01:10,020
That means going through Rwanda,
Tanzania to there.  Such a long way!

19
00:01:10,060 --> 00:01:11,700
It is a long way.  Right.

20
00:01:11,740 --> 00:01:17,900
'And so, a 600-mile journey began,
a journey that has already

21
00:01:17,940 --> 00:01:21,300
'taken us through the toughest
terrain imaginable.'

22
00:01:21,340 --> 00:01:23,700
Argh! Aaarrrrghhh!

23
00:01:23,740 --> 00:01:25,020
Whoa!

24
00:01:25,060 --> 00:01:26,900
Come on! Come on!

25
00:01:28,740 --> 00:01:31,380
AAAAARRRRRGHHHH!

26
00:01:31,420 --> 00:01:36,220
'And, on top of all this, it turned
out I was travelling with a thief.'

27
00:01:36,260 --> 00:01:39,500
How long's that been there?
Good grief!

28
00:01:39,540 --> 00:01:41,700
Is that my door?!

29
00:01:41,740 --> 00:01:44,980
Well, you're not using that bit.
You didn't even notice.

30
00:01:45,020 --> 00:01:46,260
That's it! That's it!

31
00:02:18,980 --> 00:02:23,300
'As dawn broke, the peace
and serenity of this beautiful

32
00:02:23,340 --> 00:02:28,220
'Ugandan morning was shattered
by the bellow of a wild animal.'

33
00:02:29,460 --> 00:02:31,300
CLARKSON!

34
00:02:38,020 --> 00:02:40,820
Last night, while Hammond
and May were round the campfire

35
00:02:40,860 --> 00:02:45,260
discussing Uganda, I made an
interesting modification to my car.

36
00:02:45,300 --> 00:02:48,540
Yesterday, I discovered hill starts
were very difficult.

37
00:02:48,580 --> 00:02:51,980
I can't ride the clutch because of
the throttle problem and I can't use

38
00:02:52,020 --> 00:02:54,020
the handbrake because it's broken,

39
00:02:54,060 --> 00:02:57,340
so what I've done is fitted
this log at the back.

40
00:02:57,380 --> 00:03:00,340
You tow it along normally, but then
when you want to start on a hill,

41
00:03:00,380 --> 00:03:02,140
you let the car roll back onto it,

42
00:03:02,180 --> 00:03:05,580
use it as a wedge
and set off easily.

43
00:03:05,620 --> 00:03:08,460
It's simple, it's elegant,
it's brilliant.

44
00:03:14,580 --> 00:03:19,420
'As we set off on our long drive,
the mood was a little fractious.'

45
00:03:21,180 --> 00:03:25,300
If I know James May at all,
and I'm sorry to have to say I do,

46
00:03:25,340 --> 00:03:29,620
he won't be so cross about the theft
of part of his bonnet as

47
00:03:29,660 --> 00:03:33,780
he will be about the shoddiness
of the execution of the theft.

48
00:03:33,820 --> 00:03:36,980
It's not a neat job.
That will rankle.

49
00:03:37,020 --> 00:03:38,300
Look at it!

50
00:03:38,340 --> 00:03:40,540
I mean, that looks like
it was done

51
00:03:40,580 --> 00:03:43,100
with a knife and fork
by the council.

52
00:03:43,140 --> 00:03:46,220
It's not square, it's not neat,
he's wasted material,

53
00:03:46,260 --> 00:03:49,620
he's left sharp edges,
it's all bent.

54
00:03:49,660 --> 00:03:52,660
I cannot conceive of the mind
of a man who would look at that

55
00:03:52,700 --> 00:03:55,780
and think that was
the right way to do it.

56
00:03:55,820 --> 00:03:59,020
'I don't mind you taking a bit
of my bonnet, that's fair.

57
00:03:59,060 --> 00:04:01,860
'I do mind you doing such
an appalling bloody job of it,

58
00:04:01,900 --> 00:04:03,500
'you ham-fisted oaf!'

59
00:04:03,540 --> 00:04:05,340
Honestly, I could do a better
job than that

60
00:04:05,380 --> 00:04:06,740
with the end of a bulldozer!

61
00:04:06,780 --> 00:04:09,780
Well, I...I couldn't find any tools.

62
00:04:09,820 --> 00:04:12,220
'The back of the car is full
of bloody tools!

63
00:04:12,260 --> 00:04:15,060
'The biggest tool in this
operation was you!'

64
00:04:15,100 --> 00:04:18,540
A pair of tin snips looks
like a pair of robust scissors

65
00:04:18,580 --> 00:04:22,980
and their function is obvious.
They are obviously not a hammer!

66
00:04:23,020 --> 00:04:26,820
This could go on.
'Obviously not a screwdriver!

67
00:04:26,860 --> 00:04:30,500
'They are obviously not a spanner!
They are obviously designed...'

68
00:04:30,540 --> 00:04:32,940
HE TURNS VOLUME DOWN
There we go, that's got rid of him.

69
00:04:35,940 --> 00:04:38,620
'Soon we came to an uphill stretch,

70
00:04:38,660 --> 00:04:40,780
'so I decided to test
my new handbrake.'

71
00:04:42,740 --> 00:04:46,500
Simply pop it off the back,
roll it out.

72
00:04:49,300 --> 00:04:50,420
Watch this.

73
00:04:52,060 --> 00:04:53,300
Roll back.

74
00:04:59,580 --> 00:05:04,100
The wooden handbrake is holding me,
I simply set off.

75
00:05:04,140 --> 00:05:06,060
Here we go.

76
00:05:09,980 --> 00:05:14,540
Oh, yes! So there we are,

77
00:05:14,580 --> 00:05:18,660
hill starting solved!
Sometimes my genius is...

78
00:05:19,740 --> 00:05:21,100
It's almost frightening!

79
00:05:23,300 --> 00:05:25,540
Well, fair dos, that works.

80
00:05:25,580 --> 00:05:31,100
You do have to tow a log about,
but it works. I am a happy man today.

81
00:05:33,300 --> 00:05:39,420
Chaps? Yes?  Are we likely to see a
gorilla? I hope we do see a gorilla.

82
00:05:39,460 --> 00:05:40,900
I'd show them my bonnet and say,

83
00:05:40,940 --> 00:05:42,820
"Could you do a better job
than that?"

84
00:05:42,860 --> 00:05:46,420
But look on the bright side, James,
it's going to be a scorcher today.

85
00:05:46,460 --> 00:05:48,620
SMASH!

86
00:05:49,820 --> 00:05:51,540
Oh, my God!

87
00:06:04,060 --> 00:06:09,620
RICHARD AND JAMES LAUGH
Oh! Did that..?  Oh, dear!

88
00:06:09,660 --> 00:06:13,900
The handbrake bounced up and has
broken my hotel viewing platform.

89
00:06:13,940 --> 00:06:16,740
You've got glass in your duvet!

90
00:06:16,780 --> 00:06:19,180
Stupidest idea in history!

91
00:06:19,220 --> 00:06:21,940
Jeremy,
people walk around here barefoot.

92
00:06:21,980 --> 00:06:24,780
You've got to sweep all this stuff.
We have.

93
00:06:24,820 --> 00:06:29,820
But look - God gave us a tool
for just such a moment as this.

94
00:06:29,860 --> 00:06:33,020
Would you not agree this is
the ideal tool, James, for this job?

95
00:06:33,060 --> 00:06:36,460
Yes, hammers do have their uses.
I can think of one right now.

96
00:06:37,660 --> 00:06:39,100
'After cleaning up my mess,

97
00:06:39,140 --> 00:06:44,060
'we carried on through yet more
breathtaking countryside.'

98
00:06:51,540 --> 00:06:54,380
Look at the state of that view.
Wait a minute.

99
00:06:59,860 --> 00:07:04,260
Does the world get any
prettier than this?

100
00:07:13,660 --> 00:07:17,060
'In fact the only blot
on the landscape was our hideous

101
00:07:17,100 --> 00:07:19,140
'Ford Scorpio back-up car.'

102
00:07:21,980 --> 00:07:26,020
I've just realised I forgot to eat
anything this morning. I'm starving.

103
00:07:26,060 --> 00:07:31,100
Well, Hammond made me a lovely plate
of beans.   Beans? What sort of beans?

104
00:07:31,140 --> 00:07:32,900
Do you know, they were baked?!

105
00:07:35,860 --> 00:07:40,740
'Soon, the dirt track gave way to
some perfect Chinese-built roads,

106
00:07:40,780 --> 00:07:44,300
'a blessed relief
for our battered cars.

107
00:07:52,460 --> 00:07:54,860
'And after a simple 50-mile cruise,

108
00:07:54,900 --> 00:07:56,700
'we reached the border.'

109
00:08:00,780 --> 00:08:07,060
Goodbye, Uganda, and thank you. That
was an education. And now, Rwanda.

110
00:08:07,100 --> 00:08:08,660
I don't know anything about Rwanda.

111
00:08:10,060 --> 00:08:13,540
'As it turned out,
James knew even less.'

112
00:08:16,020 --> 00:08:21,900
James? What?  Apparently they drive on
the other side of the road in Rwanda.

113
00:08:21,940 --> 00:08:27,820
Do they really(?)  James, really,
we're not fooling you! Yeah, yeah.

114
00:08:29,580 --> 00:08:31,460
BEEP!

115
00:08:39,180 --> 00:08:43,900
'Mercifully, there was more Chinese
tarmac, so we were able to surge

116
00:08:43,940 --> 00:08:46,460
'through a country where,
actually, none of us

117
00:08:46,500 --> 00:08:48,500
'had really known what to expect.'

118
00:08:51,580 --> 00:08:54,180
In 1994,
this country witnessed

119
00:08:54,220 --> 00:08:57,620
the worst genocide
in the history of humankind.

120
00:08:59,060 --> 00:09:01,900
A million people
died in around about three months.

121
00:09:03,740 --> 00:09:07,380
A million people in three months!
With machetes and garden tools.

122
00:09:09,220 --> 00:09:12,260
And that was only 18 years ago,
and now look.

123
00:09:12,300 --> 00:09:17,100
It's incredible how quickly things,
on the surface at least...

124
00:09:18,380 --> 00:09:19,340
..mend themselves.

125
00:09:24,060 --> 00:09:28,340
CHILDREN SHOUT: Hello!
Hello! Hello, hello!

126
00:09:28,380 --> 00:09:30,300
It's constant.

127
00:09:30,340 --> 00:09:32,580
All the roads,
even out here in the sticks,

128
00:09:32,620 --> 00:09:34,980
are just absolutely
three-deep in people,

129
00:09:35,020 --> 00:09:37,940
and they all wave when you go by
and you have to wave back.

130
00:09:37,980 --> 00:09:42,780
Hello! Hello!  Hello, hello.
Hello. Hello.

131
00:09:42,820 --> 00:09:44,740
Hello!   Hello.

132
00:09:51,900 --> 00:09:55,220
'After many more miles of waving,
smooth tarmac

133
00:09:55,260 --> 00:09:56,540
'and stunning scenery...

134
00:09:59,380 --> 00:10:04,020
'..we arrived on the other side of
Rwanda at its border with Tanzania.'

135
00:10:07,140 --> 00:10:10,100
That was a long drive,
but we've done it - all of Rwanda.

136
00:10:11,380 --> 00:10:14,380
Now for an interminably long wait.

137
00:10:16,260 --> 00:10:18,660
'The paperwork did indeed
take an age,

138
00:10:18,700 --> 00:10:22,100
'but, happily, Jeremy
and I found a way to pass the time.'

139
00:10:24,540 --> 00:10:26,380
Funny(!) Ha-ha-ha(!)

140
00:10:28,540 --> 00:10:31,780
'And then, finally,
we crossed over into Tanzania.'

141
00:10:33,340 --> 00:10:35,340
Shall we make camp here, gentlemen?

142
00:10:35,380 --> 00:10:39,180
It's been a long drive with a long
border crossing of 900 hours.

143
00:10:50,180 --> 00:10:52,820
'Despite the calm
stillness of the evening,

144
00:10:52,860 --> 00:10:56,660
'James plainly still had a bee
in his bonnet about his bonnet.'

145
00:10:58,300 --> 00:11:02,940
This evening
I am going to need...these.

146
00:11:02,980 --> 00:11:05,780
And I'm going to need these

147
00:11:05,820 --> 00:11:07,820
and this.

148
00:11:07,860 --> 00:11:11,460
And I'm going to need
these tin snips,

149
00:11:11,500 --> 00:11:16,340
which are like scissors but much
stronger. I'm going to need those.

150
00:11:16,380 --> 00:11:19,340
I'm going to need all these
things for this evening.

151
00:11:21,020 --> 00:11:24,260
Right, I'm up to 18 million
pieces of glass.

152
00:11:24,300 --> 00:11:27,740
Look in another fold, there you go -
18 million and two, three...

153
00:11:27,780 --> 00:11:32,380
Why do they call this stuff
safety glass? I mean, it isn't!

154
00:11:32,420 --> 00:11:34,500
Why can't it just
break into four pieces?

155
00:11:34,540 --> 00:11:38,060
Why does it have to
break into 19 million pieces?

156
00:11:38,100 --> 00:11:40,060
And it goes everywhere!

157
00:11:40,100 --> 00:11:42,340
You could be in bed
and it's outside your house

158
00:11:42,380 --> 00:11:45,140
and it goes in your pyjamas!

159
00:11:45,180 --> 00:11:47,220
18 million and ten.

160
00:11:50,540 --> 00:11:51,900
No.

161
00:11:54,540 --> 00:11:55,580
No.

162
00:12:00,540 --> 00:12:02,060
No.

163
00:12:05,420 --> 00:12:06,420
Yes.

164
00:12:09,420 --> 00:12:10,780
No.

165
00:12:45,580 --> 00:12:47,540
Good morning, viewers.

166
00:12:47,580 --> 00:12:49,980
I'm afraid things may have got
a little out of hand in camp

167
00:12:50,020 --> 00:12:52,540
last night, but it's good news!

168
00:12:52,580 --> 00:12:57,300
I found another piece of scrap metal
and I managed to mend my bonnet!

169
00:13:00,580 --> 00:13:06,180
I'll admit, I was FURIOUS when James
stole my bonnet scoop, but then

170
00:13:06,220 --> 00:13:10,500
on Jeremy's car, I found something
that does the job just as well.

171
00:13:10,540 --> 00:13:12,980
I'm calling it the poop scoop.

172
00:13:14,620 --> 00:13:20,060
I was a bit cross about losing
my lavatory, but then, staggeringly,

173
00:13:20,100 --> 00:13:24,340
in the middle of a remote field
in Tanzania, I found

174
00:13:24,380 --> 00:13:27,820
a rear window that fitted
into the back of my car!

175
00:13:27,860 --> 00:13:31,540
I mean,
it looks like it's meant to be here!

176
00:13:31,580 --> 00:13:33,460
It's uncanny!

177
00:13:35,740 --> 00:13:39,620
'Unfortunately, the modifications to
Hammond's now half-timbered Subaru

178
00:13:39,660 --> 00:13:43,620
'seem to have
impaired his visibility.'

179
00:13:43,660 --> 00:13:46,060
CRASH!
Oh!

180
00:13:46,100 --> 00:13:47,820
Oh, no!

181
00:13:49,940 --> 00:13:51,660
Bad!

182
00:13:51,700 --> 00:13:54,100
Anyway, we've woken up,
slightly thick heads.

183
00:13:54,140 --> 00:13:55,780
We're all mates again,

184
00:13:55,820 --> 00:13:59,060
which is just as well, because
we've got an extremely long day...

185
00:13:59,100 --> 00:14:00,140
Oh.

186
00:14:08,700 --> 00:14:14,900
That is a river.  Yes, it is. Well,
there must be a bridge somewhere.

187
00:14:14,940 --> 00:14:17,820
Why MUST there be a bridge? Well,
there must be!  Well, there isn't!

188
00:14:17,860 --> 00:14:21,420
Look! Well, there isn't one THERE,
but there'll be a bridge.

189
00:14:21,460 --> 00:14:26,500
I know what.
It's time to deploy the ARU.  What?

190
00:14:26,540 --> 00:14:29,540
The ARU -
Aerial Reconnaissance Unit.

191
00:14:29,580 --> 00:14:32,780
I knew this sort of thing would
happen.   Where did that come from?

192
00:14:32,820 --> 00:14:35,740
I've been working on it for
the last couple of weeks at home

193
00:14:35,780 --> 00:14:39,660
for just such an eventuality.
Have you?  Have you really?

194
00:14:39,700 --> 00:14:43,940
Did you build it? Yeah.  Really? Well,
you've got a helicopter licence.

195
00:14:43,980 --> 00:14:45,580
You fly it.  Right.

196
00:14:45,620 --> 00:14:47,420
I operate the camera underneath

197
00:14:47,460 --> 00:14:51,580
that swivels about and you tell us
what it's seeing, James.

198
00:14:54,540 --> 00:14:57,140
Right, are we ready?
Yes, I've got a picture.

199
00:14:57,180 --> 00:14:59,180
Hammond?  Yes... Begin!

200
00:15:04,500 --> 00:15:07,500
Yes! There she goes!
There you go. That's good.

201
00:15:09,140 --> 00:15:12,380
Right, that height is good.
Now head that way, down the river.

202
00:15:12,420 --> 00:15:16,020
Point it down. You're not pointing
it at the river. Point it down.

203
00:15:16,060 --> 00:15:17,860
Well, you'll need to point it down.

204
00:15:17,900 --> 00:15:20,100
No, you fly level,
you point the camera down.  Yeah.

205
00:15:22,580 --> 00:15:24,580
Got it. River turns left...
That's good.

206
00:15:24,620 --> 00:15:26,500
There's no bridge, though.
Keep going.

207
00:15:28,060 --> 00:15:29,100
Pivot right a bit.

208
00:15:30,700 --> 00:15:32,500
Swivel left, camera.

209
00:15:35,980 --> 00:15:38,060
Oh, hang on a minute.   What?

210
00:15:38,100 --> 00:15:41,300
There's like a big weir thing.
It's not a weir, James!

211
00:15:41,340 --> 00:15:44,260
That's the biggest waterfall
I've ever seen!

212
00:15:44,300 --> 00:15:47,100
Right, that's relevant(!)
Right, good.  No bridge.

213
00:15:48,740 --> 00:15:51,820
'And it was the same story
when we looked upstream.'

214
00:15:54,100 --> 00:15:55,420
Bring it back, Hammond.

215
00:15:57,580 --> 00:15:59,420
'With the ARU back at base...

216
00:16:01,580 --> 00:16:04,940
'..we had to work out how
we could cross this river

217
00:16:04,980 --> 00:16:09,300
'that was full of killer waterfalls,
bilharzia and many teeth.'

218
00:16:14,380 --> 00:16:20,420
Why don't we build...a car ferry?
What, with like a bar and everything?

219
00:16:20,460 --> 00:16:22,860
No, no!
Like that thing we saw in Albania!

220
00:16:22,900 --> 00:16:25,900
Like a raft?  Why don't WE build one?

221
00:16:25,940 --> 00:16:28,740
Look, I used to build them when I
was a kid! I fell in a lot, but...

222
00:16:28,780 --> 00:16:34,140
Wood... What else do you need?
Rope.   Oil drums.

223
00:16:34,180 --> 00:16:37,460
You need to lash the rope together.
Maybe some nails and a hammer.

224
00:16:37,500 --> 00:16:40,540
Probably a hammer, yeah.
Oil drums, oil drums underneath.

225
00:16:40,580 --> 00:16:42,740
This sounds like the worst
management course ever!

226
00:16:42,780 --> 00:16:46,500
You know those Outward Bound courses
where you've got a chicken and...

227
00:16:46,540 --> 00:16:48,940
OK, then. Thanks to Hammond,
it's back to the studio.

228
00:16:48,980 --> 00:16:50,100
All we need is a platform

229
00:16:50,140 --> 00:16:52,900
big enough to take a car that
will take its weight and floats.

230
00:16:52,940 --> 00:16:55,460
Then we pull it across on ropes
like the Albanian one.

231
00:16:55,500 --> 00:16:57,380
We drove through a village,
did we not,

232
00:16:57,420 --> 00:16:59,660
about...just behind those
banana trees?

233
00:16:59,700 --> 00:17:01,860
Yes, but do you think
they sell rafts for cars?

234
00:17:01,900 --> 00:17:04,700
They might sell car ferries. No,
but they've always got...

235
00:17:04,740 --> 00:17:08,460
Don't want a banana, don't want
a headscarf, I'd like a car ferry.

236
00:17:08,500 --> 00:17:11,780
"Oh, we've got one in the back, sir."
Look, I'm going to divide this up.

237
00:17:11,820 --> 00:17:15,980
I'm going to find some rope. You go
and find some oil drums. You go...

238
00:17:16,020 --> 00:17:18,380
I'll get some wood.   And some wood.

239
00:17:18,420 --> 00:17:23,340
MUSIC: AFRICAN-SOUNDING VERSION
OF THEME FROM THE A-TEAM

240
00:17:26,060 --> 00:17:28,980
Right, off you go. Have you got
the hammer and stake?   Yeah.

241
00:17:30,820 --> 00:17:32,300
Oh!

242
00:17:37,140 --> 00:17:39,100
Your rope's come off!

243
00:17:42,340 --> 00:17:45,060
You're getting nearer, James!
I'm not!

244
00:17:48,220 --> 00:17:49,420
Oh.

245
00:17:49,460 --> 00:17:52,460
Would you say you were nearer,
James?   No.

246
00:18:00,980 --> 00:18:03,620
'With HMS Wobbly in the water,

247
00:18:03,660 --> 00:18:07,220
'Hammond and May decided that
my car should go first.'

248
00:18:12,980 --> 00:18:17,060
Good. A tiny, tiny bit left.
Straight, that's good.

249
00:18:17,100 --> 00:18:19,060
Hold on.

250
00:18:19,100 --> 00:18:22,300
You need to go... Oh,  BLEEP.
It's close. Tiny bit right.

251
00:18:24,780 --> 00:18:29,700
OK, your back wheels are on.
I am now boarding our ferry.

252
00:18:29,740 --> 00:18:34,220
That's good.   'As I inched forwards,
a hungry audience gathered.'

253
00:18:37,100 --> 00:18:39,340
You'll feel when you're on
cos you'll hit the chock.

254
00:18:41,420 --> 00:18:44,020
Two and a half feet.   Feet?!   Yeah.

255
00:18:46,740 --> 00:18:50,460
Good, a bit more. You're just
about to... There you go, you're on!

256
00:18:50,500 --> 00:18:51,740
Put it in gear and turn off.

257
00:18:54,220 --> 00:18:56,140
These have got to come with us
for the other side.

258
00:18:58,300 --> 00:19:01,660
'With the ramps on board,
we set off.'

259
00:19:01,700 --> 00:19:04,540
Whoa! Whoa, whoa, whoa, whoa, whoa.

260
00:19:04,580 --> 00:19:07,020
What did you just do?   Cast off.

261
00:19:07,060 --> 00:19:11,380
Are we now underway?   Yes.
Pull on the rope.   I am.

262
00:19:11,420 --> 00:19:13,060
I'm Geoff Capes!

263
00:19:13,100 --> 00:19:17,580
I am pulling a BMW 528i Touring
across a crocodile-infested river!

264
00:19:17,620 --> 00:19:20,980
And the impressive thing is, you're
not making a fuss about it either.

265
00:19:21,020 --> 00:19:23,220
No, I'm not showing off, but I am...

266
00:19:23,260 --> 00:19:24,860
James, wait! James, James, James!

267
00:19:24,900 --> 00:19:26,700
What? It doesn't make any
difference.

268
00:19:26,740 --> 00:19:28,980
Stay at the back!
James, please stay at the back!

269
00:19:29,020 --> 00:19:31,780
Stop being such an old woman -
it doesn't make any difference!

270
00:19:31,820 --> 00:19:34,820
Oh, really? So that barrel's just
come back above the water again...

271
00:19:34,860 --> 00:19:37,460
But it's not going to go
all the way down, is it?

272
00:19:37,500 --> 00:19:39,300
(I feel like an explorer now.)

273
00:19:40,540 --> 00:19:43,940
Right...  So if I get on the bank
and James throws me a yellow rope...

274
00:19:43,980 --> 00:19:46,180
Good idea.
Can you get on the bank from there?

275
00:19:46,220 --> 00:19:49,460
These logs get really slippery! Yes.
Right, you're on the bank...

276
00:19:51,260 --> 00:19:52,300
Good catch(!)

277
00:19:52,340 --> 00:19:55,220
The throw wasn't brilliant.
I'll go to the back. Whey!

278
00:19:57,380 --> 00:19:58,500
Well...

279
00:20:00,860 --> 00:20:03,860
People are now turning over to watch
Red Or Black? or something.

280
00:20:03,900 --> 00:20:06,260
I'm sorry we made it.

281
00:20:06,300 --> 00:20:07,740
Nobody was expecting that.

282
00:20:07,780 --> 00:20:08,740
Got it?

283
00:20:08,780 --> 00:20:12,780
'Mind you, there was still the small
matter of disembarking.'

284
00:20:12,820 --> 00:20:16,060
Uh...

285
00:20:16,100 --> 00:20:17,580
..not convinced.

286
00:20:17,620 --> 00:20:19,540
Yes!

287
00:20:23,620 --> 00:20:25,340
Well, you're NEARLY ashore.

288
00:20:25,380 --> 00:20:27,020
Aren't I ashore?

289
00:20:27,060 --> 00:20:29,060
Not entirely ashore, no.

290
00:20:29,100 --> 00:20:31,140
Yes, I'm ashore.

291
00:20:31,180 --> 00:20:32,900
Is that wheel ashore? Yes.

292
00:20:32,940 --> 00:20:35,140
'We weren't convinced,

293
00:20:35,180 --> 00:20:37,140
'so we gave him a helping hand.'

294
00:20:37,180 --> 00:20:38,180
Here we go!

295
00:20:38,220 --> 00:20:40,100
Yes!

296
00:20:40,140 --> 00:20:41,340
Feeling good.

297
00:20:41,380 --> 00:20:44,060
Oh, yes, oh, yes!

298
00:20:44,100 --> 00:20:46,180
God, I'm a good driver.

299
00:20:46,220 --> 00:20:47,740
(Oh, God.)

300
00:20:51,700 --> 00:20:53,820
'Back on the other bank,

301
00:20:53,860 --> 00:20:55,940
'it was Hammond's turn,
and for some reason,

302
00:20:55,980 --> 00:20:58,500
'he decided to make life difficult
for himself.'

303
00:20:58,540 --> 00:21:00,740
Why are you BACKING on?

304
00:21:00,780 --> 00:21:04,820
Because it is quite difficult getting
off at the other side, yeah? Yeah.

305
00:21:04,860 --> 00:21:07,380
And your BMW is now there
so it could tow me off.

306
00:21:07,420 --> 00:21:09,060
The only towing eye is at the back.

307
00:21:09,100 --> 00:21:11,420
It does mean I'll have to
reverse on.

308
00:21:13,180 --> 00:21:15,820
Hold on, hold on!
Does that look right from there?

309
00:21:15,860 --> 00:21:17,620
What...?

310
00:21:17,660 --> 00:21:20,220
Oh!

311
00:21:23,940 --> 00:21:26,340
Oh! Stop! Stop!
You're turning the wheel.

312
00:21:26,380 --> 00:21:29,580
I did NOT turn the wheel.
You turned it minutely.

313
00:21:29,620 --> 00:21:31,420
I might have leant on it.

314
00:21:31,460 --> 00:21:33,660
Oh!

315
00:21:33,700 --> 00:21:35,860
You're miles off the end.
A little more.

316
00:21:35,900 --> 00:21:37,380
And...stop.

317
00:21:37,420 --> 00:21:39,580
Oh! I am SO onboard!

318
00:21:39,620 --> 00:21:42,700
James, you can pull this time,
you'll see how easy it is(!)

319
00:21:42,740 --> 00:21:45,660
I shall be ballast, there.
That's the ticket!

320
00:21:45,700 --> 00:21:48,940
Not all at the same time.
You can't come on this side.

321
00:21:48,980 --> 00:21:51,100
I can't go on the other side.

322
00:21:51,140 --> 00:21:53,740
Look at the angle.
Gentlemen...  Climb through the car.

323
00:21:53,780 --> 00:21:56,180
..It is impossible to stand there

324
00:21:56,220 --> 00:21:59,180
because there are two ramps piled
on top of one another.

325
00:21:59,220 --> 00:22:00,860
Hammond, get in your sitting room!

326
00:22:00,900 --> 00:22:02,700
I can't.
Get in your sitting room!  No!

327
00:22:02,740 --> 00:22:04,100
You need to be on the other side.

328
00:22:04,140 --> 00:22:06,940
Climb over the bloody car.

329
00:22:06,980 --> 00:22:10,020
You've got heavier...  Oh!

330
00:22:11,260 --> 00:22:15,100
That was tied on! No, I took
the rope off.   We needed the rope.

331
00:22:15,140 --> 00:22:16,660
I took it off.

332
00:22:16,700 --> 00:22:19,140
I CAN go on the other side...

333
00:22:19,180 --> 00:22:20,580
as it turns out.

334
00:22:20,620 --> 00:22:22,580
Perfect. Let's go. Forget the chair.

335
00:22:26,580 --> 00:22:28,060
Are we there yet(?)

336
00:22:28,100 --> 00:22:30,060
Somebody's had me chair!

337
00:22:30,100 --> 00:22:31,540
That man's got your chair!

338
00:22:31,580 --> 00:22:34,660
Good for him. Enjoy it!

339
00:22:34,700 --> 00:22:36,020
Ooh, ah!

340
00:22:36,060 --> 00:22:39,860
'On the other side, I was determined
to make a better job of disembarking

341
00:22:39,900 --> 00:22:41,340
'than Jeremy had.'

342
00:22:43,460 --> 00:22:44,940
Power.  Hoo!

343
00:22:44,980 --> 00:22:46,980
Some of you's onboard.

344
00:22:47,020 --> 00:22:48,740
Whoa, we got a problem.

345
00:22:51,260 --> 00:22:54,860
That's all completely hopeless
and you've sunk the raft

346
00:22:54,900 --> 00:22:56,860
and your catflap's terrible.

347
00:22:58,620 --> 00:22:59,780
GO!

348
00:22:59,820 --> 00:23:01,500
'I then pulled Hammond off...'

349
00:23:01,540 --> 00:23:03,420
Ya-hey!

350
00:23:03,460 --> 00:23:05,860
'..And that gave me an idea.'

351
00:23:05,900 --> 00:23:09,100
Why don't we use this to pull
the ferry across

352
00:23:09,140 --> 00:23:10,540
with the Volvo on it?

353
00:23:13,420 --> 00:23:15,140
Why would we not do that?

354
00:23:16,860 --> 00:23:21,100
'Rather sceptically, Hammond and I
went back to get my Volvo.'

355
00:23:21,140 --> 00:23:24,540
Crack on.

356
00:23:24,580 --> 00:23:26,300
Our father, who art in heaven,

357
00:23:26,340 --> 00:23:29,020
if you're there,
make it go upside down, please.

358
00:23:31,900 --> 00:23:34,940
Steady! Wooh!

359
00:23:34,980 --> 00:23:37,660
Ooh! (That looked a bit perilous.)

360
00:23:37,700 --> 00:23:40,580
Shall I come back?  Just a tiny bit.

361
00:23:40,620 --> 00:23:43,860
Wah-duh...! Stop making
squeaking noises.

362
00:23:43,900 --> 00:23:47,900
The thing is, the BMW has
a 50-50 weight distribution.

363
00:23:47,940 --> 00:23:50,740
50% over the front axle,
50% over the back,

364
00:23:50,780 --> 00:23:53,180
which is why the raft was level.

365
00:23:53,220 --> 00:23:54,660
With James' car,

366
00:23:54,700 --> 00:23:58,020
70% of the weight is over
the front axle.

367
00:23:58,060 --> 00:24:00,500
That's why the whole thing
is tipping down.

368
00:24:00,540 --> 00:24:02,340
Are we attached? Yes.

369
00:24:02,380 --> 00:24:05,300
Jeremy, we have begun!

370
00:24:05,340 --> 00:24:08,020
Right,
so I pull you across the river.

371
00:24:08,060 --> 00:24:09,460
Yeah, gently.

372
00:24:09,500 --> 00:24:12,300
Very, very slowly indeed!

373
00:24:13,700 --> 00:24:16,140
If it goes under,
it'll probably go nose-in, won't it?

374
00:24:16,180 --> 00:24:20,460
Yeah, I'll stand at the back.
Are you ready?

375
00:24:20,500 --> 00:24:22,660
He's going already, hang on.

376
00:24:22,700 --> 00:24:25,060
This is Captain Hammond
ringing down to the engine room.

377
00:24:25,100 --> 00:24:28,540
You have the slacks.
That's plenty fast enough!

378
00:24:29,980 --> 00:24:32,580
Yes, gently.

379
00:24:32,620 --> 00:24:35,660
Seriously, Jeremy.
We are going under.

380
00:24:35,700 --> 00:24:37,660
That's too fast, too fast!

381
00:24:37,700 --> 00:24:39,700
Oh, yes!

382
00:24:39,740 --> 00:24:40,740
Oh, yes!

383
00:24:42,100 --> 00:24:43,780
Gently, gently - seriously!

384
00:24:43,820 --> 00:24:45,100
We're going in.

385
00:24:45,140 --> 00:24:46,620
STOP!

386
00:24:46,660 --> 00:24:51,460
Jeremy, absolutely stop
or you'll have deaths on your hands.

387
00:24:51,500 --> 00:24:54,940
I've been shouted at so much.

388
00:24:54,980 --> 00:24:56,580
Need a zesty drink.

389
00:25:02,300 --> 00:25:08,940
'Eventually, our third and final car
was ready to go ashore.'

390
00:25:08,980 --> 00:25:11,420
Forward.  Oh, my Gawd!

391
00:25:13,140 --> 00:25:16,020
Well, credit where it's due.

392
00:25:16,060 --> 00:25:17,900
Come on!

393
00:25:19,980 --> 00:25:22,860
You have to admit that is quite
a finish, with a flourish!

394
00:25:25,180 --> 00:25:29,460
'It had been a fraught undertaking,
but the fact was that,

395
00:25:29,500 --> 00:25:31,100
'against the odds,

396
00:25:31,140 --> 00:25:35,380
'all three of our cars were
on the other side of the river.'

397
00:25:35,420 --> 00:25:38,180
That's the first time,
I think, in ten years,

398
00:25:38,220 --> 00:25:39,460
we've ever done anything

399
00:25:39,500 --> 00:25:41,100
ambitious and successful.

400
00:25:41,140 --> 00:25:43,660
I think you're right.
And doesn't it feel good?

401
00:25:43,700 --> 00:25:45,580
It feels weird, frankly.

402
00:25:45,620 --> 00:25:48,340
Normally there's one of us
in the water, in the jaws of a lion,

403
00:25:48,380 --> 00:25:50,020
going, "Oh, he's dead!"

404
00:25:50,060 --> 00:25:51,420
It was genuinely peculiar.

405
00:25:51,460 --> 00:25:53,060
Oh, my God!

406
00:25:53,100 --> 00:25:55,380
Oh!

407
00:25:55,420 --> 00:25:57,780
THEY LAUGH

408
00:25:57,820 --> 00:26:00,540
It's gone! It's completely gone!

409
00:26:00,580 --> 00:26:01,860
It's completely sunk!

410
00:26:01,900 --> 00:26:03,820
Well done(!)

411
00:26:03,860 --> 00:26:05,740
You can see how difficult
OUR job is.

412
00:26:05,780 --> 00:26:07,940
That's how deep it was!

413
00:26:07,980 --> 00:26:10,060
That could have happened to us.

414
00:26:10,100 --> 00:26:12,540
It could have done...
BOTH: but it didn't!

415
00:26:12,580 --> 00:26:14,100
BOTH: Onwards.

416
00:26:14,140 --> 00:26:17,180
'Leaving the producers to
clear up their mess,

417
00:26:17,220 --> 00:26:18,620
'we set off.

418
00:26:22,540 --> 00:26:25,420
'And soon discovered that
Tanzania's roads were not

419
00:26:25,460 --> 00:26:26,740
'built by the Chinese.'

420
00:26:28,420 --> 00:26:32,620
Pothole, pothole, pothole,
pothole. BIG one.

421
00:26:32,660 --> 00:26:35,740
It is like driving through
a minefield.

422
00:26:35,780 --> 00:26:39,620
If you take your eye
off the road for a second,

423
00:26:39,660 --> 00:26:42,380
you are into one
and they are massive.

424
00:26:45,100 --> 00:26:47,340
I don't know how James
will cope with this,

425
00:26:47,380 --> 00:26:50,260
because you can only
concentrate on one thing at a time

426
00:26:50,300 --> 00:26:55,380
and when he's driving that is
normally an obscure poet.

427
00:26:55,420 --> 00:26:58,460
"Oh, yes, Philip Larkin's done some
very good...oh, no,

428
00:26:58,500 --> 00:27:00,380
"I've hit a pothole."

429
00:27:00,420 --> 00:27:02,500
'Soon James hit a pothole...'

430
00:27:02,540 --> 00:27:04,140
THUMP

431
00:27:04,180 --> 00:27:05,980
No!

432
00:27:06,020 --> 00:27:10,020
'..So hard,
it took out two of his tyres.'

433
00:27:10,060 --> 00:27:13,220
Erm...d'you remember...

434
00:27:13,260 --> 00:27:15,100
it seems like months ago,

435
00:27:15,140 --> 00:27:18,340
but at the beginning of this voyage,
we looked at these tyres and said,

436
00:27:18,380 --> 00:27:20,820
"There's no way those tyres'll
survive Africa?"

437
00:27:20,860 --> 00:27:23,100
The signs are not good, are they?

438
00:27:23,140 --> 00:27:25,660
In that they're not. It's goodbye.

439
00:27:25,700 --> 00:27:28,300
You just wait here for the backup
car.  You know the code.

440
00:27:28,340 --> 00:27:29,740
Oh, no, the backup car's

441
00:27:29,780 --> 00:27:32,020
at the bottom of a
crocodile-infested river.

442
00:27:32,060 --> 00:27:34,180
I don't need the backup car,
I have spare wheels.

443
00:27:34,220 --> 00:27:37,020
You're not very helpful or useful
to me. I'll see you later.

444
00:27:37,060 --> 00:27:39,060
See you, James. Bye.  Bye!

445
00:27:40,340 --> 00:27:42,740
'Once the Annoying Brothers
had left,

446
00:27:42,780 --> 00:27:46,660
'I discovered it was rather more
than just tyre damage.'

447
00:27:46,700 --> 00:27:49,460
Ho-ho-ho!

448
00:27:49,500 --> 00:27:52,660
'Both wheels had shattered!

449
00:27:56,380 --> 00:27:58,980
'Having raided the support
truck for more,

450
00:27:59,020 --> 00:28:00,980
'I set off and caught up
with the others

451
00:28:01,020 --> 00:28:03,220
'in a swarm of lake flies.'

452
00:28:06,100 --> 00:28:08,220
FLIES BUZZING

453
00:28:11,580 --> 00:28:15,380
'This told us we were getting
close to Lake Victoria.

454
00:28:15,420 --> 00:28:18,860
'At 150 miles across,

455
00:28:18,900 --> 00:28:21,740
it was too wide for us to even think
about building a homemade ferry.

456
00:28:21,780 --> 00:28:25,820
'So we decided to use one someone
else had made earlier.'

457
00:28:28,660 --> 00:28:31,060
Have you ever seen more
flies than that

458
00:28:31,100 --> 00:28:33,100
in one place ever?

459
00:28:33,140 --> 00:28:34,700
Oh, oh, oh!

460
00:28:36,940 --> 00:28:38,980
I'll give you a million pounds
if you go

461
00:28:39,020 --> 00:28:40,900
and stand there naked for an hour.

462
00:28:44,900 --> 00:28:48,140
PIANO PLAYS CLAIR DE LUNE

463
00:29:20,060 --> 00:29:22,860
'The voyage across Lake Victoria
took more than

464
00:29:22,900 --> 00:29:26,620
'16 hours, but there was
good news on the other side.

465
00:29:26,660 --> 00:29:29,260
'The ferry would drop us
close to the River Grumeti,

466
00:29:29,300 --> 00:29:31,580
'the river that WE believed
would take us

467
00:29:31,620 --> 00:29:34,340
'to the source of the Nile.'

468
00:29:36,820 --> 00:29:39,260
BELL RINGS

469
00:29:39,300 --> 00:29:41,580
We're going to find
the mouth of the river,

470
00:29:41,620 --> 00:29:44,380
track that back to its source,

471
00:29:44,420 --> 00:29:45,940
and we've done it.

472
00:29:50,540 --> 00:29:51,780
30 seconds...

473
00:29:58,660 --> 00:30:01,420
'Keen to demonstrate
the advantage of four-wheel drive

474
00:30:01,460 --> 00:30:03,660
'to the bewildered crowd,

475
00:30:03,700 --> 00:30:06,500
'Hammond insisted on going first.'

476
00:30:06,540 --> 00:30:08,940
Right, here we go.

477
00:30:21,620 --> 00:30:23,180
OK.

478
00:30:26,860 --> 00:30:28,020
I am ashore!

479
00:30:41,140 --> 00:30:43,900
OVER RADIO: 'You're letting
yourself down, the BBC,'

480
00:30:43,940 --> 00:30:46,940
and the whole country with your
incompetence, Hammond.

481
00:30:49,100 --> 00:30:50,940
Oh, come on!

482
00:30:52,420 --> 00:30:55,100
Sorry about that, Skipper!

483
00:30:55,140 --> 00:30:57,700
Leave him.   We can leave him.

484
00:30:57,740 --> 00:30:59,940
'And so...

485
00:30:59,980 --> 00:31:01,260
'we did.'

486
00:31:02,780 --> 00:31:04,420
Bye, Hammond! Bye!

487
00:31:07,100 --> 00:31:08,820
Hello.

488
00:31:08,860 --> 00:31:09,860
Hello.

489
00:31:13,180 --> 00:31:16,980
'As we disembarked a few miles away
on a proper landing jetty,

490
00:31:17,020 --> 00:31:19,660
'Hammond was being
rescued by the locals.'

491
00:31:19,700 --> 00:31:21,900
SHOUTING AND CHEERING

492
00:31:23,500 --> 00:31:25,180
We're coming out!

493
00:31:30,620 --> 00:31:32,660
Everybody needs money now

494
00:31:32,700 --> 00:31:34,700
and I don't have any money.

495
00:31:34,740 --> 00:31:36,220
Erm...

496
00:31:38,380 --> 00:31:40,180
I've got biscuits.

497
00:31:41,700 --> 00:31:44,380
More biscuits, more sweets.

498
00:31:44,420 --> 00:31:45,620
One second.

499
00:31:47,740 --> 00:31:50,140
OK, this is becoming alarming.

500
00:31:50,180 --> 00:31:52,580
Erm...

501
00:31:52,620 --> 00:31:55,180
I've got crisps.

502
00:31:57,980 --> 00:32:01,900
'With my lunch all gone,
I rejoined my colleagues.'

503
00:32:04,660 --> 00:32:07,380
With hindsight, offering up
biscuits was a mistake.

504
00:32:07,420 --> 00:32:09,740
HE LAUGHS

505
00:32:09,780 --> 00:32:12,460
Yes,
when you have 40 mouths to feed,

506
00:32:12,500 --> 00:32:15,500
unless you're Jesus,
starting with two biscuits

507
00:32:15,540 --> 00:32:16,740
isn't brilliant.

508
00:32:18,700 --> 00:32:20,980
Well, that arrival
was very happy and glorious

509
00:32:21,020 --> 00:32:23,220
and we're excellent
ambassadors for Britain

510
00:32:23,260 --> 00:32:25,260
and her long tradition
of exploration.

511
00:32:25,300 --> 00:32:27,300
In other news,

512
00:32:27,340 --> 00:32:29,540
I've fitted a fan.

513
00:32:34,340 --> 00:32:38,780
'We then broke out the map and went
off to find the mouth of our river.

514
00:32:41,140 --> 00:32:44,500
'The beautiful and majestic
Grumeti.'

515
00:32:52,940 --> 00:32:55,620
In my mind, it was prettier. Yes.

516
00:32:55,660 --> 00:32:57,740
This is quiet, though.
It's not quiet.

517
00:32:57,780 --> 00:33:01,900
I admit, it's not picturesque
and it's not peaceful,

518
00:33:01,940 --> 00:33:04,380
but if we find the source of this...

519
00:33:04,420 --> 00:33:05,860
We've done it.

520
00:33:05,900 --> 00:33:07,940
..You can't argue,
we're in the history books.

521
00:33:07,980 --> 00:33:10,340
It flows through Lake Victoria,
into the Nile,

522
00:33:10,380 --> 00:33:11,940
along the Nile,

523
00:33:11,980 --> 00:33:14,020
through the Med
and into the Atlantic.

524
00:33:14,060 --> 00:33:15,980
And as far as I can work out,

525
00:33:16,020 --> 00:33:17,660
it's there.

526
00:33:17,700 --> 00:33:19,980
That's about 70 miles.

527
00:33:20,020 --> 00:33:23,180
We only have 70 miles to do.
The good news is, look, some of it,

528
00:33:23,220 --> 00:33:25,380
you can follow the river
on that road.

529
00:33:25,420 --> 00:33:28,620
But that bit, the last bit,

530
00:33:28,660 --> 00:33:31,460
it's off-road.  Yeah.

531
00:33:31,500 --> 00:33:34,820
Look, there's no tracks or anything.

532
00:33:34,860 --> 00:33:37,820
Well... So, that's it.
It can only get prettier.

533
00:33:40,820 --> 00:33:44,860
'And it did, because soon
we were in the Serengeti.

534
00:33:53,260 --> 00:33:56,340
'A vast plain teaming with animals,
all of which, as usual,

535
00:33:56,380 --> 00:34:00,060
'were way beyond the reach
of our camera teams.'

536
00:34:05,220 --> 00:34:07,220
Wildebeest! Wildebeest!

537
00:34:10,380 --> 00:34:12,500
A zebra.

538
00:34:18,060 --> 00:34:21,260
I've seen more already here
on the Serengeti

539
00:34:21,300 --> 00:34:23,700
than I saw in
Sir Richard Hammondborough's

540
00:34:23,740 --> 00:34:25,500
Planet Earth Live programme.

541
00:34:25,540 --> 00:34:28,780
ON RADIO: 'Why didn't you show us
all of these animals, Hammond,

542
00:34:28,820 --> 00:34:30,900
'on your Planet Earth Live
programme?

543
00:34:30,940 --> 00:34:33,300
'There's millions of them
we could have looked at!'

544
00:34:33,340 --> 00:34:34,860
Shut up!

545
00:34:38,220 --> 00:34:41,780
'Soon, we stopped to check on the
course of our "beautiful" river.

546
00:34:41,820 --> 00:34:46,420
'And here we saw the most
amazing wildlife yet.'

547
00:34:49,740 --> 00:34:53,060
They are the funniest creatures
on earth, aren't they?

548
00:34:55,860 --> 00:34:57,620
Why are they so funny?

549
00:35:00,460 --> 00:35:02,940
This one's been to
the Daktari shop in Florida,

550
00:35:02,980 --> 00:35:04,260
hasn't he, for his outfit?

551
00:35:04,300 --> 00:35:06,380
Every single piece of kit,
he's got the lot.

552
00:35:06,420 --> 00:35:08,980
I don't know why,
but American tourists -

553
00:35:09,020 --> 00:35:12,420
and I know we're watched in America,
I'm not saying you're all like this,

554
00:35:12,460 --> 00:35:14,620
but when you travel,
you're hysterical.

555
00:35:14,660 --> 00:35:16,660
THEY LAUGH

556
00:35:16,700 --> 00:35:20,500
'Sadly, the Americans departed,
leaving us with nothing to look at

557
00:35:20,540 --> 00:35:22,580
'except some hippos.

558
00:35:22,620 --> 00:35:25,940
'So we got back on the road
for the last few miles

559
00:35:25,980 --> 00:35:28,940
'of our epic journey.'

560
00:35:31,780 --> 00:35:35,180
I'm going to put my hand
on my heart and say this

561
00:35:35,220 --> 00:35:37,820
is the best Top Gear adventure
we've ever had.

562
00:35:37,860 --> 00:35:40,660
It's a noble quest in a truly

563
00:35:40,700 --> 00:35:45,340
stunningly beautiful country
full of utterly hospitable people.

564
00:35:45,380 --> 00:35:48,940
'And then, of course,
there were our cars.

565
00:35:50,140 --> 00:35:52,780
'A trio of 1,500-quid high-milers.

566
00:35:54,660 --> 00:35:56,500
'All had been presumably sold

567
00:35:56,540 --> 00:36:00,380
'because their owners thought
they were on their last legs,

568
00:36:00,420 --> 00:36:04,180
'but they'd come here and
taken on the worst

569
00:36:04,220 --> 00:36:07,060
'that Africa could throw at them,
and they'd survived.

570
00:36:09,140 --> 00:36:11,620
'But which had been the most
impressive?

571
00:36:11,660 --> 00:36:15,540
'Well, at the next river check,
we had a chat about that.'

572
00:36:16,660 --> 00:36:18,860
Normally we pick one car
that's best.

573
00:36:18,900 --> 00:36:20,980
Mm.   I think on this occasion,

574
00:36:21,020 --> 00:36:23,740
I can't be convinced mine
isn't the best,

575
00:36:23,780 --> 00:36:25,700
But I'm sure you're in the same...

576
00:36:25,740 --> 00:36:29,020
I wouldn't allow anybody to
convince me mine isn't the best.

577
00:36:29,060 --> 00:36:29,860
So, in other words,

578
00:36:29,900 --> 00:36:32,420
let's, on this occasion,
just agree to disagree

579
00:36:32,460 --> 00:36:34,300
and say they're all the best.  Yes.

580
00:36:34,340 --> 00:36:36,740
Like one of those primary school
sports days.

581
00:36:36,780 --> 00:36:38,860
Yes. Everybody gets a prize.

582
00:36:38,900 --> 00:36:41,900
You've all won.
There are no losers at St Barnabas'.

583
00:36:41,940 --> 00:36:44,420
Let's go with that,
they are all the best.

584
00:36:44,460 --> 00:36:46,580
That's fair.
They've all done well together.

585
00:36:46,620 --> 00:36:50,140
Let us now find the source of
this disgusting, scummy river.   OK.

586
00:36:52,220 --> 00:36:53,980
Rubbish!

587
00:36:54,020 --> 00:36:57,340
If this WERE a school sports day,
here's how it's worked out.

588
00:36:57,380 --> 00:37:00,500
Everyone's got all excited
because the BMW, the fat kid,

589
00:37:00,540 --> 00:37:03,500
and the Volvo, the geeky,
specky, nerdy kid,

590
00:37:03,540 --> 00:37:05,580
have finished the cross-country
course.

591
00:37:05,620 --> 00:37:08,900
"Oh, well done,
we're so amazed you did it!"

592
00:37:08,940 --> 00:37:11,460
But let's not forget
the fact that the genuinely

593
00:37:11,500 --> 00:37:14,300
sporty kid, the Subaru,
who's actually good at this stuff,

594
00:37:14,340 --> 00:37:17,340
also finished and finished well.

595
00:37:17,380 --> 00:37:19,940
Because it's the best. Fact.

596
00:37:25,060 --> 00:37:27,140
Ten minutes after this
show's finished,

597
00:37:27,180 --> 00:37:29,460
you won't be able to describe
what Jeremy was driving,

598
00:37:29,500 --> 00:37:31,780
but you'll be able to describe
this...

599
00:37:31,820 --> 00:37:34,620
because it's got personality,
character,

600
00:37:34,660 --> 00:37:37,180
something about it.

601
00:37:37,220 --> 00:37:39,700
I'm going to miss it.

602
00:37:41,780 --> 00:37:44,780
Now we're alone, viewers,
I can tell you that the Volvo

603
00:37:44,820 --> 00:37:47,620
is the best car here.
Because, let's not forget,

604
00:37:47,660 --> 00:37:49,860
this is a family estate.

605
00:37:49,900 --> 00:37:55,340
It's a family estate pretending
to be a BTCC racing car.

606
00:37:55,380 --> 00:37:57,060
So it's compromised, as well.

607
00:37:57,100 --> 00:37:59,660
It shouldn't be here,
it shouldn't have got this far,

608
00:37:59,700 --> 00:38:01,460
but it is and it has,

609
00:38:01,500 --> 00:38:03,660
and that's why I love it!

610
00:38:03,700 --> 00:38:05,700
It has the biggest heart.

611
00:38:07,980 --> 00:38:09,780
How can May possibly say

612
00:38:09,820 --> 00:38:11,780
that his Volvo's better than this?

613
00:38:11,820 --> 00:38:14,620
It's been like a seal
on the entire journey,

614
00:38:14,660 --> 00:38:17,940
endlessly dragging its stomach
along the floor.

615
00:38:17,980 --> 00:38:20,340
And it broke his back.

616
00:38:20,380 --> 00:38:22,940
He'd have been better off
doing this journey

617
00:38:22,980 --> 00:38:24,220
on a space hopper.

618
00:38:24,260 --> 00:38:26,620
Then we have Hammond's "Tubaru,"

619
00:38:26,660 --> 00:38:28,780
which is as needlessly complicated

620
00:38:28,820 --> 00:38:32,100
as those idiotic trousers
he insists on wearing

621
00:38:32,140 --> 00:38:34,820
with all their special pockets
and clips for mossie spray

622
00:38:34,860 --> 00:38:37,580
and a hunting knife
with a special compass.

623
00:38:37,620 --> 00:38:38,940
What's the point?

624
00:38:38,980 --> 00:38:43,740
I've done the entire journey
in a pair of jeans and a t-shirt.

625
00:38:43,780 --> 00:38:46,540
And that's what the 5 Series is,

626
00:38:46,580 --> 00:38:48,900
it's the familiarity of home...

627
00:38:48,940 --> 00:38:50,300
here.

628
00:38:53,620 --> 00:38:57,300
You're a car, you're a sitting-room,
you're a bedroom, you're a fridge,

629
00:38:57,340 --> 00:39:00,780
you're a power station that charges
up my phone every night,

630
00:39:00,820 --> 00:39:04,380
but most of all what you are,
what you've become -

631
00:39:04,420 --> 00:39:07,140
is a mate.

632
00:39:07,180 --> 00:39:10,100
And that is what makes
a car special.

633
00:39:10,140 --> 00:39:11,700
That's what makes a car great.

634
00:39:11,740 --> 00:39:14,420
You start to think of it
as a person.

635
00:39:16,300 --> 00:39:17,620
You start to love it.

636
00:39:19,260 --> 00:39:23,500
'What our cars deserved now was
a relaxing cruise to the finish.

637
00:39:23,540 --> 00:39:25,140
'But that wasn't going to happen.'

638
00:39:27,180 --> 00:39:29,380
Aaaagh! Aaaagh! No!

639
00:39:31,260 --> 00:39:32,820
Whoa! Geez!

640
00:39:34,700 --> 00:39:38,300
'The road was a rutted nightmare,

641
00:39:38,340 --> 00:39:40,940
'and it was peppered
with sharp stones.

642
00:39:42,460 --> 00:39:44,300
'It was a car killer.'

643
00:39:45,420 --> 00:39:48,060
This is absolute
puncture alley, this.

644
00:39:50,540 --> 00:39:51,940
Bloody Nora!

645
00:39:55,500 --> 00:39:57,220
BANG!

646
00:39:57,260 --> 00:39:58,900
What the  BLEEP was that?

647
00:39:58,940 --> 00:40:03,100
Now, Jez has stopped at the side of
the road, hang on, something is up.

648
00:40:03,140 --> 00:40:06,340
Jezza's been hit, I think,
probably by a stone into his window.

649
00:40:06,380 --> 00:40:08,740
Stone in through your window?

650
00:40:08,780 --> 00:40:11,380
Something has just blown up
in there.

651
00:40:13,060 --> 00:40:14,700
What the  BLEEP was it?

652
00:40:16,900 --> 00:40:19,740
That... Is it an airbag?
The airbag has gone off.

653
00:40:19,780 --> 00:40:24,420
Look, the smoke's still pouring
out of it.  Is that the airbag?

654
00:40:24,460 --> 00:40:27,660
Yes, the airbag just went off.
There was a massive flash...

655
00:40:27,700 --> 00:40:30,100
That is really weird!   My ear.

656
00:40:30,140 --> 00:40:32,140
I was saying this road
is so rough it's going

657
00:40:32,180 --> 00:40:34,980
to set the airbags off,
I said that just a few miles back.

658
00:40:35,020 --> 00:40:37,740
Well, I have never seen that.
No, me neither.

659
00:40:41,020 --> 00:40:42,620
Phwoar!

660
00:40:44,580 --> 00:40:46,340
Now I'm worried about this one.

661
00:40:48,020 --> 00:40:51,860
'By now we'd had enough
and wanted to stop for the night,

662
00:40:51,900 --> 00:40:55,340
'but this was dusk on the Serengeti.

663
00:40:55,380 --> 00:40:58,340
'Feeding time for the wildlife.

664
00:40:58,380 --> 00:41:01,740
'So we had to reach a safe camp
that was 30 miles away -

665
00:41:01,780 --> 00:41:05,460
'30 miles that
turned into a massacre.'

666
00:41:09,860 --> 00:41:11,580
Oh, dear. Shot to bits.

667
00:41:15,300 --> 00:41:18,460
Have you got any more fronts?
Yes, I've got one more.

668
00:41:22,420 --> 00:41:24,740
That was new three minutes ago.

669
00:41:24,780 --> 00:41:27,180
Three minutes later I've
got a flat on the front.

670
00:41:33,020 --> 00:41:34,900
We've lost another one.

671
00:41:34,940 --> 00:41:38,980
This road is beyond belief
for killing cars.

672
00:41:39,020 --> 00:41:43,140
'It was inevitable, really,
that eventually one of us

673
00:41:43,180 --> 00:41:46,300
'would suffer some damage
that was slightly more serious

674
00:41:46,340 --> 00:41:47,460
'than a puncture.'

675
00:41:48,780 --> 00:41:51,620
Oh, my God. You are in big trouble.

676
00:41:51,660 --> 00:41:54,740
That one is straight, and
if you come around the other side,

677
00:41:54,780 --> 00:41:56,460
that's on full-left.

678
00:41:56,500 --> 00:42:00,300
You've broken a track rod
steering...  Oh, my God.

679
00:42:00,340 --> 00:42:02,580
You can see the wishbone...there.

680
00:42:02,620 --> 00:42:07,020
If you look in there you'll see
the end of it, where it's...gone.

681
00:42:07,060 --> 00:42:09,860
Is it sheared?  Yes. It is
completely sheared off.

682
00:42:09,900 --> 00:42:11,460
That might have killed my car.

683
00:42:13,340 --> 00:42:17,540
What's up?   He's got a puncture.
He's also got a sheared wishbone.

684
00:42:17,580 --> 00:42:19,220
You're kidding.   I'm not.

685
00:42:20,460 --> 00:42:22,700
That's a game over, isn't it?

686
00:42:35,300 --> 00:42:37,260
'The next morning, in our safe camp,

687
00:42:37,300 --> 00:42:39,900
'we counted up the toll
from Death Road.'

688
00:42:42,620 --> 00:42:45,700
I had three punctures,
one of which I have still got.

689
00:42:45,740 --> 00:42:47,700
Two airbags went off,

690
00:42:47,740 --> 00:42:51,500
both of which deafened me
and gave me heart attacks.

691
00:42:51,540 --> 00:42:54,740
What else - oh, all four shock
absorbers are completely ruined.

692
00:42:54,780 --> 00:42:56,780
Yours any better?

693
00:42:56,820 --> 00:43:00,220
Well, I had two punctures,
the fuel line came off,

694
00:43:00,260 --> 00:43:02,060
the front bumper collapsed,

695
00:43:02,100 --> 00:43:04,340
I am not even bothering
to look at my shocks,

696
00:43:04,380 --> 00:43:07,660
because I know they were
absolutely shot, shot absorbers,

697
00:43:07,700 --> 00:43:11,020
but on the plus side, all
the bits that attach the wheels

698
00:43:11,060 --> 00:43:12,420
to the car are intact.

699
00:43:13,500 --> 00:43:15,700
Speaking of which...

700
00:43:15,740 --> 00:43:17,620
actually, where is he?

701
00:43:17,660 --> 00:43:19,420
BANGING

702
00:43:26,060 --> 00:43:29,660
{\an8}These are the remnants of the point
at which the track control arm went,

703
00:43:29,700 --> 00:43:33,740
and you can see it was just
rotten from the inside out.

704
00:43:33,780 --> 00:43:37,140
It was going to go at some point,
and when it did collapse,

705
00:43:37,180 --> 00:43:40,260
it took the anti-roll bar
out on the way past.

706
00:43:40,300 --> 00:43:44,580
So, the only option is,
and it is quite a long shot -

707
00:43:44,620 --> 00:43:46,180
this is all useless -

708
00:43:46,220 --> 00:43:49,460
is to use the remainder of the
track control arm that's under

709
00:43:49,500 --> 00:43:50,740
there attached to the hub,

710
00:43:50,780 --> 00:43:55,340
and use this to fabricate and weld
on replacement bits for that.

711
00:44:00,180 --> 00:44:03,020
'At times like this,
the procedure is clear.

712
00:44:03,060 --> 00:44:05,620
'We do leave a man behind.

713
00:44:05,660 --> 00:44:09,300
'But, because we'd come
so far together, and because

714
00:44:09,340 --> 00:44:15,020
'we hadn't had breakfast yet, James
and I decided to bend the rules.'

715
00:44:15,060 --> 00:44:16,780
We'll give him till 11 o'clock.

716
00:44:18,260 --> 00:44:19,500
Fair enough.

717
00:44:19,540 --> 00:44:21,900
That's an hour and ten minutes.
Fair enough.

718
00:44:21,940 --> 00:44:24,260
What time did we get in last night,
about two?

719
00:44:24,300 --> 00:44:26,420
Something like that,
it went on for ever.

720
00:44:26,460 --> 00:44:28,620
Look at what I just slept in,
look at it!

721
00:44:30,620 --> 00:44:32,660
I mean look at the...   Thank you(!)

722
00:44:43,260 --> 00:44:45,900
'At exactly three minutes past 11,

723
00:44:45,940 --> 00:44:49,340
'James and I were parked on the main
road by the camp, waiting to see

724
00:44:49,380 --> 00:44:51,740
'whether we would finish
this journey as a twosome,

725
00:44:51,780 --> 00:44:53,980
'or a threesome.'

726
00:44:54,980 --> 00:44:58,420
Hammond is making a bit of a habit
of this on these adventures, now.

727
00:44:58,460 --> 00:45:01,300
Bolivia, his leg fell off.   Oliver.

728
00:45:01,340 --> 00:45:04,140
Oliver? Didn't it make it?
Can't remember.

729
00:45:04,180 --> 00:45:06,700
It did, but we had to wait for
it...   Oh, yes, for days and days.

730
00:45:06,740 --> 00:45:08,620
..while he put it back together.

731
00:45:12,900 --> 00:45:15,100
Oh, hang on!

732
00:45:15,140 --> 00:45:17,740
Do you hear that?   I can, just.

